BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2026-10-14_1758_facebook_first-10-minutes-with-ai.md@social-squad-sched
 ule
DTSTAMP:20261002T095800Z
DTSTART:20261002T095800Z
DTEND:20261002T102800Z
SUMMARY:Facebook: Your first 10 minutes with AI (free\, nothing to install)
DESCRIPTION:published - You've heard that everyone is using AI. You've mayb
 e made an account. And now you're looking at an empty box\, wondering what
  a "good" question even looks like\, and whether you're about to do someth
 ing dumb.\n\nYou're not. There's nothing to break here\, and it costs noth
 ing. Only 34% of workers say their employer offers any AI training\, so mo
 st people are teaching themselves. This is the shortest version of that I 
 know.\n\n**Step 1: Pick one real task.**\nSomething from your actual week 
 that you mildly dislike. A tricky email\, a meeting summary\, a formula yo
 u can never remember. Don't pick "learn AI." Pick a task.\n\n**Step 2: Ope
 n a free tool.**\nClaude\, ChatGPT and Gemini all have free plans. Make an
  account with your email. Cost is the barrier 57% of people name\, so star
 t on the free plan and stay there until you have a reason to leave it.\n\n
 **Step 3: Strip out anything confidential.**\nThis is the step people skip
 \, so it comes before your first prompt. A free tool is a company's server
 \, and what you type goes there. Depending on the tool and the plan\, chat
 s may be reviewed or used to improve the model. Defaults differ\, so open 
 the privacy or data settings in your account and read what yours does.\n\n
 Then build one habit. Before you paste anything\, swap client and colleagu
 e names for placeholders ("Client A")\, round the numbers\, and delete wha
 tever identifies a deal\, a person or a price. Keep the shape of the probl
 em and lose the details that point to a real one.\n\nSome things never go 
 in at all: passwords\, contracts\, anything your company has told you to k
 eep in-house. If your employer has an AI policy\, follow it. If you're not
  sure\, ask\, or use a made-up example. Worry about privacy is one of the 
 top reasons people hold back from AI\, and it's a fair worry. This step is
  how you answer it.\n\n**Step 4: Give it a situation\, then a request.**\n
 An empty prompt gets you a generic answer. Try this one\, with your own de
 tails changed:\n\n*"I work in finance. Here's a draft email to my manager 
 about a budget overrun on Project X: [paste\, with names and exact figures
  changed]. Make it shorter and calmer\, and keep the numbers."*\n\nYour si
 tuation first\, your request second. That's the whole formula.\n\n**Step 5
 : Push back.**\nSay "too formal." Say "put the number first." Say "give me
  a version for someone who's annoyed." The first answer is a draft\, and t
 he skill you're building is steering it.\n\n**Step 6: Check it.**\nAI writ
 es confident sentences\, including wrong ones. Read every number and every
  claim against your source before you send anything. Then put your real na
 mes and figures back in yourself. This takes two minutes and it keeps you 
 out of trouble.\n\n## Free videos to watch next\n\nWatching helps\, especi
 ally if you follow along with your own task open in another window. Search
  these channels:\n\n- **Anthropic (official):** walkthroughs of Claude fro
 m the people who make it.\n- **OpenAI (official):** the same for ChatGPT.\
 n- **Google (official):** intro videos on Gemini.\n\nCheck the dates on wh
 atever you click. These tools change quickly\, and a video from a year ago
  may show a different screen.\n\n## What's next\n\nYou've now used AI on r
 eal work\, safely. The problem is that it lives in a chat window and disap
 pears. In Part 2\, I'll set up VS Code and GitHub step by step so you can 
 keep your work\, save every version\, and let AI help inside your own file
 s. Under an hour\, and every step stands on its own.\n\nCheers!
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
